there's some encouraging news in the  race to find a coronavirus vaccine a new  study  study from oxford university says its  experimental vaccine  prompted an immune response in hundreds  who received the shot  a key sign of progress charlie dagetta  spoke to the scientist leading the  trials  america's one billion dollar bet on the  oxford vaccine just got a lot closer to  paying off  professor sarah gilbert what's the most  important takeaway from what we've  learned today  that the vaccine is safe and it's giving  us strong immune responses and it should  go into further clinical trials  the strong immune response they'd hoped  for the deployment of antibodies  and crucial killer t cells antibodies  detect a virus and neutralize it  t cells are more like assassins not only  attacking  infected human cells but retaining a  memory of the virus ready to mount a  defense  the next time the same pathogen tries to  invade  what are the biggest challenges left now  that phase one  and phase two are complete or underway  what's what's next  looking at the performance of the  vaccine in older people is really  important  we know that most vaccines don't give  such strong immune responses as the  immune system  ages the trump administration has put  1.2 billion dollars on the line  for drug giant astrazeneca to produce at  least 300 million doses as soon as  they're ready to go  in an online briefing astrazeneca ceo  pascal sorio says  they're on track our hope is that  we can actually start delivering a  vaccine  by before the end of the year professor  gilbert  is a bit more measured pascal sorrio  said this afternoon he's still hoping  for like october november  well we all have hopes there are no  guarantees  i mean i don't want to put a date on it  because it's just impossible to tell  we're working as hard as we can to do  everything we need to do as fast as we  can  and we'll get the result when we get the  result  in fact things are moving much faster  than at any time  in history from identifying a pathogen  to getting this  close to developing a workable vaccine  work here started back in january in  what they call their  little lab here at oxford university now  the entire world is watching and waiting  tony so far so good charlie thank you  very much
 

